<!--
  Every user-visible string in the app.

  Why this file exists: the screen copy used to live inline in the Compose
  sources, next to the condition that decided whether it was shown. That was a
  deliberate trade for a single-language app, but it made translation impossible
  without editing Kotlin — so the strings moved here, and `values-zh/` now holds
  the Chinese translation of this same key set.

  Conventions
  ===========
  * Keys are prefixed by where they are used: `screen_`, `section_`, `target_`,
    `reject_`, `warn_`, `key_`, `node_`, `test_`, `step_`, `diag_`, `net_`,
    `status_`, `web_`, plus `provider_` for names that are owned by the domain
    layer.
  * Resource *ids* travel through `@StringRes Int`, never display text: the
    domain layer returns an id, the UI resolves it. That is what keeps the
    domain free of Android context while still being translatable.
  * Placeholders use positional `%n$s` form, so a translation may reorder them.
    Text inside <xliff:g> is never translated — it is a value, not a word.
  * Keep the key set identical to `values-zh/strings.xml`. A key present in one
    file and missing from the other silently falls back to English.
-->
